# Swift 6 concurrency rules

This project compiles with Swift 6.0, `SWIFT_DEFAULT_ACTOR_ISOLATION = MainActor` (SE-0466 "Approachable Concurrency"), and strict data-race checking. Read this every time you edit a `.swift` file. For deep dives (TaskGroup design, AsyncSequence, reentrancy, migration from `@preconcurrency`), also load the global skill at `~/.claude/skills/swift-concurrency/SKILL.md`.

## What "default MainActor isolation" means here

- Top-level types, properties, and functions are implicitly `@MainActor` unless you opt out. Don't add `@MainActor` to a SwiftUI `View`, an `@Observable` view model, or a regular `final class` / `struct`.
- Explicit annotations aren't wrong; just don't add them reflexively. Don't remove existing explicit `@MainActor` without a reason.
- To run off the main actor, opt out deliberately (next section).

## Off-main-actor patterns (in order of preference)

1. `nonisolated` synchronous helper: pure functions with no shared state. Cheapest, no executor hop.
2. `@concurrent` async function on a `nonisolated` type: genuine background work (parsing, hashing, file IO that doesn't need the main actor).
3. A custom `actor`: serialized mutable state that is not UI-bound (e.g., an in-memory cache).
4. `Task.detached`: last resort, almost never needed here. Justify it in a code comment.

`Task { ... }` started inside MainActor-isolated code inherits MainActor and runs on the main thread, which is usually right for SwiftUI work (e.g., kicking off an async fetch from `.task { ... }`). Don't add `@MainActor in` to such a closure; it is redundant.

## Sendable rules for this codebase

- New value types should be `Sendable` by construction (immutable `let` properties of `Sendable` types). Prefer `struct` over `class`.
- Mark protocols that cross isolation boundaries `Sendable`. Canonical example: `AnalyticsClient: AnyObject, Sendable` in `simple-recurring-budgets/Logging/AnalyticsClient.swift`.
- `@unchecked Sendable`, `nonisolated(unsafe)`, and `@preconcurrency` are forbidden without an inline justification. If you must use one, add a `// concurrency: ...` comment naming the invariant that makes it safe.
- `[String: any Sendable]` is the right shape for analytics event payloads (see `AddEditBudgetViewModel.budgetEventProperties`). Don't widen to `Any`.

## SwiftData and `ModelContext`

- `ModelContext` is not `Sendable`. Never capture it in a `@concurrent` function or a `Task.detached`, or store it on a non-MainActor type.
- View models don't store `ModelContext` (see `.cursor/rules/swift-ios.mdc` §Screen architecture). Writing methods take `(context: ModelContext, ...)` at the call site, on the MainActor.
- For background SwiftData work, use a `@ModelActor` type and pass IDs (`PersistentIdentifier`) across the boundary, never model instances or contexts.

## SwiftUI specifics

- `View.body`, `.task`, `.onAppear`, `.onChange` are MainActor-isolated. `Task { ... }` inside them stays on the main actor.
- For background work from a view: call a `@concurrent` async function on a domain service, then assign results on return (you are back on the main actor).
- Don't annotate `View` types with `@MainActor`; it is implied.

## Common mistakes to avoid

- ❌ Adding `@MainActor` to every type "to be safe." The default already handles it.
- ❌ `Task.detached { @MainActor in ... }`: pointless hop. Use `Task { ... }` from a MainActor caller.
- ❌ Capturing `self` in a `@concurrent` closure when `self` is MainActor-isolated. Capture only the `Sendable` values you need.
- ❌ Making a protocol `Sendable` without auditing every conformance; adopters silently take on the constraint.
- ❌ Using `@unchecked Sendable` to silence a warning. Fix the unsafe sharing.
- ❌ Wrapping synchronous work in `Task { ... }` for no reason. If the work is sync and on-main is fine, leave it sync.

## Authoring workflow (mandatory)

After any Swift edit, before reporting completion, run the four-step procedure from `AGENTS.md > Build and test`:

1. `make format`
2. `make lint-fix`
3. `make build` (the concurrency gate; must pass clean)
4. `make test`

If `make build` reports actor-isolation, `Sendable`, or data-race errors, don't silence them with `@unchecked Sendable`, `nonisolated(unsafe)`, or `@preconcurrency`. Fix the isolation. If you genuinely need an unsafe escape hatch, add a `// concurrency:` comment explaining the invariant and ask the user to confirm before committing.

